Why use Frameworks?
Reduces repetitive coding
Organizes project structure
Easier maintenance
Faster development
Better scalability
Popular Java Frameworks : Spring

2. What is Spring Framework?

Spring is an open-source Java framework used for building:
Web Applications
REST APIs
Enterprise Applications
Cloud Applications
Microservices

Goal of Spring

Make Java development:
1. Simpler
2. Faster
3. Easier to Test
4. Easier to Maintain

Important Concepts of Spring

1. IoC (Inversion of Control)
Normally:
Engine e = new Engine();
-->Programmer creates objects.
With Spring:
Car c = context.getBean(Car.class);
-->Spring creates and manages objects.

2. Dependency Injection (DI)
Dependency :
class Car{
   Engine engine;
}
-->Car depends on Engine.

Without DI :
Engine engine = new Engine();
-->Creates Tight Coupling.

With DI :
@Autowired
Engine engine;
-->Spring injects the object automatically.

Advantages:
Loose Coupling
Easy Testing
Reusability
Better Maintenance

Spring IoC Container :

--> The IoC Container is the heart of Spring.
Responsibilities :
Creates Objects (Beans)
Manages Lifecycle
Injects Dependencies
Configures Applications

Types of IoC Containers :

1. BeanFactory
-->Basic container.
Features :
Lazy Initialization
Lightweight
Less Memory Usage
Disadvantages :
Limited Features
Rarely Used

2. ApplicationContext
-->Advanced container.
Features :
Eager Initialization
Event Handling
Internationalization
AOP Support
Most Commonly Used Container

What is a Bean?
--> A Java object managed by Spring.
Example:
        @Component
        class Student{}

Bean Lifecycle :

Step 1:
Bean Creation
Student s = new Student();

Step 2:
Dependency Injection
Dependencies are injected.

Step 3:
Initialization
@PostConstruct
Runs after bean creation.

Step 4:
Bean Ready
Used by application.

Step 5:
Destruction
@PreDestroy
Runs before bean removal.

Types of Dependency Injection
1. Constructor Injection:
   @Autowired
   public Car(Engine engine)
   {
    this.engine = engine;
   }
Advantages:
Mandatory Dependency
Immutable Objects
Better Testing
Avoids NullPointerException

2. Setter Injection
   @Autowired
   public void setEngine(Engine engine)
   {
    this.engine = engine;
   }
--> Used for optional dependencies.

3. Field Injection
   @Autowired
   private Engine engine;
--> Simple but not preferred.

Autowiring : Spring automatically injects dependencies.
| Type        | Meaning               |
| ----------- | --------------------- |
| byType      | By data type          |
| byName      | By bean name          |
| constructor | Constructor injection |
| annotation  | Using @Autowired      |

Spring MVC :

MVC = Model View Controller
Model --> Contains data and business logic.
View --> Displays data to user.
Controller --> Handles requests.

Flow of Spring MVC :
1. User sends request
2. Controller receives request
3. Service processes logic
4. Repository accesses database
5. Response returned
6. View displayed

Layers :
Client
   ↓
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
Database

What are Microservices?
--> Large application divided into small services.
--> Each service works independently.
Example:
User Service
Payment Service
Order Service

Spring Boot : Spring Boot simplifies Spring development.

Important Spring Annotations
| Annotation     | Purpose                |
| -------------- | ---------------------- |
| @Component     | Create Bean            |
| @Controller    | MVC Controller         |
| @Service       | Business Logic         |
| @Repository    | Database Layer         |
| @Autowired     | Inject Dependency      |
| @Qualifier     | Select Specific Bean   |
| @Primary       | Default Bean           |
| @Bean          | Create Bean Manually   |
| @Configuration | Configuration Class    |
| @PostConstruct | Initialization         |
| @PreDestroy    | Destruction            |
| @Transactional | Transaction Management |
